# Choosing a design, and turning an estimate into a policy
subtitle: "How to choose a causal design by the promise you can defend, and what more must be argued before an estimate can price a policy."
tags: ["causal inference","research design","health policy","medicaid","marginal value of public funds","cost-effectiveness"]
teaches: ["methods/research-design","methods/external-validity","methods/welfare-analysis"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}

## Which design can your data buy?
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: mcclellan1994
      title: Does more intensive treatment of acute myocardial infarction in the elderly reduce mortality? Analysis using instrumental variables
      authors: McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse, JAMA
      year: 1994
      finding: Distance to hospital as an instrument for heart-attack treatment

    shape f1 thick #8f887c shape rect x 188 y 520 width 350 height 300 radius 14 hidden true
    text n1 "Randomized trial" x 188 y 575 font_size 46 hidden true
    text p1 "draw was random" #4a443e x 188 y 460 font_size 38 hidden true
    shape f2 thick #8f887c shape rect x 562 y 520 width 350 height 300 radius 14 hidden true
    text n2 "Instrument" x 562 y 575 font_size 46 hidden true
    text p2 "only via coverage" #4a443e x 562 y 460 font_size 38 hidden true
