    text n6 "Observables" x 937 y 245 font_size 46 hidden true
    text p6 "nothing unmeasured" #4a443e x 937 y 130 font_size 38 hidden true
    group card1 members ["f1","n1","p1"] hidden true
    group card2 members ["f2","n2","p2"] hidden true
    group card3 members ["f3","n3","p3"] hidden true
    group card4 members ["f4","n4","p4"] hidden true
    group card5 members ["f5","n5","p5"] hidden true
    group card6 members ["f6","n6","p6"] hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["f1","n1","p1","f2","n2","p2","f3","n3","p3","f4","n4","p4","f5","n5","p5","f6","n6","p6"] hidden true

    card title "Which design can your data buy?"

A state wants to know whether covering (@draw f2@) more low-income adults with  (@draw f3@)Medicaid is worth what it cos (@draw f4@)ts: which design should answ (@draw f5@)er that, and what must the an (@draw f6@)swer become before it prices anything?
    draw f1

The tempting answer is to rank the designs by prestige, a rand (@point at.ref f6 gesture circle@)omized trial first, and take the best one on offer.
    highlight f1

But you rarely get to choose: the design is handed to you by the way coverage happened to vary in your data.
    highlight f1 f2 f3 f4 f5 f6

If someone drew names at random, as Oregon did for Medicaid in 2008, you have a ra (@draw p1@)ndomized trial, and the one promise is that t (@highlight f1@)he draw was random and both groups were followed alike.
    draw n1

If something random only nudges people toward coverage, like a lottery win that merely lets  (@highlight f2@)you apply, that nudge is an instrument.
    draw n2

Then you promise more: the nudge changes health only through coverage, (@point at.ref p2 gesture underline@) and it never pushes anyone away from coverage.
    draw p2

Which makes Oregon's draw both at once: a trial of the invitation, and an instrument for coverage itself, and only the second reading needs those two extra promises.
    highlight f1 f2

If some states expanded Medicaid in 2014 and others did not, you compare thei (@draw p3@)r changes, a difference in differences, an (@highlight f3@)d promise both would have trended alike without it.
    draw n3

If coverage jumps at a rule's cutoff, as Medicare does at sixty-five, (@draw p4@) you compare people just either side,  (@highlight f4@)and promise that nothing else jumps there too.
    draw n4

If only one state changed, as California did with its 1988 tobacco tax, you weight o (@draw p5@)ther states into a blend that tracks it before (@highlight f5@)hand, and promise the blend would have kept tracking it.
    draw n5

And if all you have is who chose coverage, with their age, income and heal (@draw p6@)th, you compare like with like, and promi (@highlight f6@)se that nothing you did not measure still differs.
    draw n6

Now read the promises in order: the first is kept by a coin, the last by n (@highlight f6@)othing but your argument about what you measured.
    highlight f1

So the rule is not the most prestigious design, but the one whose promise you can defend to someone who would like it to fail.
    highlight f1 f2 f3 f4 f5 f6

    ask "Heart-attack patients who live nearer a hospital with a catheter lab are more likely to get the procedure, and few people choose where to live for their heart. Click the design." intro "A new case." widget click answer card2
        right: An instrument: distance nudges who gets the procedure, as McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse used it in 1994, and the promise is that distance changes survival only through the procedure.
        wrong: Is distance the treatment itself, or something close to random that pushes people toward it?

And that promise is a hard one, because living near a big hospital may bring faster ambulances and other care as well.
    highlight f2

Whichever card you hold, what comes out is the same kind of thing: one number, for some people, in one place and time.
    fade card2 card3 card4 card5 card6 to 0.3

So take the strongest card, Oregon's lottery, and follow how much more must be argued before its number can price a policy.
    highlight f1

## Whose effect is it?
