    card title "A price on the estimate"

Suppose the number now belongs to the right people: a budget office still asks what each dollar buys.
    draw frac

The simplest answer is a cost per outcome: the extra cost, call it delta C, divided by the change in the outcome, delta Y.
    point at.ref frac at.anchor bottom gesture circle

On the cost side, Finkelstein, Hendren and Luttmer put Oregon's Medicaid spending at  (@point at.ref c_gross gesture tap@)about three thousand six hundred dollars per person-year.
    draw c_gross c_cite

But about sixty cents of each of those dollars paid for care the uninsured already got without paying, so the net cos (@fade c_gross to 0.35@)t, extra care plus bills people no longer paid themse (@point at.ref c_net gesture tap@)lves, is about fourteen hundred and fifty.
    draw c_net

On the outcome side, the share of people screening  (@draw dep_mark dep_lab b_cite@)positive for depression fell by about nine points, from about thirty percent.
    draw ax xlab ty50 ty100 parallel true

Divide one by the other, and each person-year free of depression costs roughly sixteen thousand dollars, if all of the cost is charged to depression.
    draw price pt ro_e ro_r ro_u

That reading assumes the effect holds in every covered year; the nine points were mea (@point at.ref dep_mark gesture circle@)sured after about two years, and if two years of coverage bought them, the p (@point at.ref ro_r gesture underline@)rice is about twice as high.
    point at.ref ro_u gesture underline

But the nine points came with a range: anything from about two points to about seventeen fits the data.
    draw band band_lo band_hi

Grab the point on the curve and slide it along, from one end of the depression range to the other, and watch the price per case.
    explore params ["d"] watch "Watch the price per case as the effect slides from one end of its range to the other."

At seventeen points the price falls under nine thousand dollars.
    animate d 16.7 duration 2

At two, it climbs to about ninety thousand.
    animate d 1.6 duration 2.5

A ratio magnifies the uncertainty in its denominator, most of all when that denominator could lie close to zero.
    animate d 9.15 duration 2

And three choices hide in (@erase dep_lab band_lo band_hi@) this one division, none of them estimated by any design.
    point at.ref frac gesture circle

Which cost? Count the full three thousand six hundred the state pays, and the price more than doubles, to about thirty-nine thousand.
    curve gross dashed thick #8f887c expr 360/x x_from 3.6 x_to 20
    text gross_lab "if $3,600" #8f887c at.data [14.5,35] font_size 30

Which outcome? Charging everything to depression ignores the fall in catastrophic medical bills, and the rise in diabetes diagnoses.
    point at.ref frac at.anchor bottom gesture circle

And which yardstick? To set depression beside a hip replacement you convert it to quality-adjusted life yea (@draw ch_w@)rs, with weights from surveys of how people value a year in that state.
    point at.ref pt gesture circle

Then a threshold, which someone has to choose, turns the price into a yes or a no.
    draw ch_t

    quiz "Which of these could a larger Oregon sample change?"
        intro: Sort the estimate from the choices.
        right: Only the range: more people shrink the sampling uncertainty, while the other two are choices no sample makes.
        wrong: Which of these is estimated from the lottery's people, and which is picked by the analyst?
        * Whether to count $3,600 or $1,448
        * The weight on a year of depression
        + The range around the nine points

So a cost per outcome is half estimate and half choice, and both halves can move it a long way.
    highlight band

## Worth it, per dollar?
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: hsk2020
      title: A Unified Welfare Analysis of Government Policies
      authors: Hendren and Sprung-Keyser, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2020
      finding: Oregon Medicaid MVPF 1.16 (95% CI 1.08 to 1.25; Table II, NBER WP 26144); 0.50 to 1.16 across specifications; 1993 EITC expansion 1.12.
    - id: fhl2019
      title: 'The Value of Medicaid: Interpreting Results from the Oregon Health Insurance Experiment'
      authors: Finkelstein, Hendren and Luttmer, Journal of Political Economy
      year: 2019
      finding: Recipient WTP $1,675 (SE 60) to $793 (SE 417) per year; net cost $1,448, gross $3,600; EITC MVPF about 0.9 (Hendren 2016).
    - id: mjw2021
      title: 'Medicaid and Mortality: New Evidence from Linked Survey and Administrative Data'
      authors: Miller, Johnson and Wherry, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2021
      finding: ACA expansions cut annual mortality of low-income adults 55-64 by 0.132 points (SE 0.050), 9.4%.
takeaways:
    - The data choose the design
    - The estimate belongs to compliers
    - 'Pricing adds choices: name them'

