    card title "Worth it, per dollar?"

A price per case cannot say whether the same dollars would do more good elsewhere, so a second yardstick ask (@draw mv@)s what a program is worth to the people who get it, per dollar it costs.
    draw mv_lab

Hendren and Sprung-Keyser call it the marginal value of public funds: W, what recipients would pay for the program, over C, its cost to the government net of anything it saves.
    point at.ref mv gesture circle

Above one, recipients val (@point at.ref t1 gesture underline@)ue the program at more than it costs; (@point at.ref t05 gesture underline@) below one, at less.
    draw axis t0 t05 t1 t15 one parallel true

For Oregon, Finkelstein, Hendren and Luttmer built W from the lottery's own estimates, of what coverage did to spend (@point at.ref mv gesture circle@)ing, to out-of-pocket bills and to health, since no one could simply be asked.
    morph mv tex "\\frac{\\$1{,}675}{\\$1{,}448} = 1.16"

Valuing each effect directly gives about sixteen hundred and seventy-five dollars a year, over a net cost of about fourteen hundred and fifty that counts the sixty cents as m (@highlight a_pt@)oney the public gets back: a ratio of 1.16.
    draw a_pt

That is Oregon's figure in Hendren and Sprung-Keyser's comparison of policies, with a ninety-five percent interval from 1.08 to 1.25.
    draw a_ci a_lab a_cite

But a second approach, which infers the value from how recipients' other spending moves with their med (@point at.ref mv gesture circle@)ical bills, gives seven hundred and ninety-three dollars, with a standard error of about four hundred.
    morph mv tex "\\frac{\\$793}{\\$1{,}448} = 0.55"

That is a ratio  (@draw b_pt b_ci b_lab b_cite@)near 0.55, with a range so wide it reaches from zero past one.

The interval around 1.16 is narrow; the distance between the two approaches is  (@point at.ref b_pt gesture circle@)far wider, and no larger lottery would ever close it.
    point at.ref a_ci gesture underline

And the denominator hides a choice of its own: the state actually pays the full three thousand six hundred.
    morph mv tex "\\frac{\\$1{,}675}{\\$3{,}600} = 0.47"

Put that in the denominator and treat the sixty cents as worth nothing to anyone, and the ratio falls to about 0.47.
    draw c_lo c_lo_lab

But if whoever gets those sixty cents, the hospitals or poorer patients, values them dollar for dollar, they belong on t (@draw c_line c_hi c_hi_lab@)op as well, and the ratio is back near one, at 1.06.
    morph mv tex "\\frac{\\$1{,}675 + \\$2{,}152}{\\$3{,}600} = 1.06"

For comparison, Hendren estimated in 2016 that a dollar of the Earned Income Tax Credit is worth about ninety cents to the families who get it; his later work with Sprung-Keyser puts the 1993 expansion at 1.12.
    draw eitc eitc_lab

So whether Oregon's Medicaid beats a cash credit turns less on sampling error than on an approach and an incidence that someone has to argue.
    highlight a_pt b_pt c_lo c_hi eitc

And all of these figures leave out what Oregon was too small to see: effects on mortality and on most of physical health.
    draw mort_box mort_line_1

Studies of the 2014 expansions, by Miller, Johnson and Wherry, found yearly mortality among low-income adults aged fifty-five to sixty-four fell by about 0.13 points, with a standard error of 0.05, roughly nine percent.
    draw mort_line_2 mort_line_3

If that holds, the value per dollar is understated; longer follow-up of the lottery's people, linked to death records, is what would settle it.
    erase mort

    quiz "A job program costs the government $1,000 per person. Recipients would pay $500 for it, and it later brings back $500 in extra taxes. What is its MVPF?" intro "One more case."
        right: One: recipients value it at five hundred, and after the extra taxes the government is out five hundred too.
        wrong: Take what the program brings back off its cost before you divide.
        * 2.0
        * 0.5
        + 1.0

So which design should answer the state's (@move strip scale 0.5 to.x 330 to.y 230 duration 1.5@) question? The one whose promise you can (@draw takeaway_1@) defend, and a lottery makes that promise almost free.
    erase mv_lab mv a_lab a_cite b_lab b_cite c_lo_lab c_hi_lab eitc_lab

Its number then belongs to the people the lottery moved, in one state and one year, and carrying it anywhere else is a second argument.
    draw takeaway_2

Pricing it is a third: costs and effects come from the design, but which cost, which outcome and which yardstick are chosen, a (@highlight a_pt c_lo c_hi@)nd they can move the answer further than its interval.
    draw takeaway_3

So report the estimate with its interval, and every choice beside it with what it does; then those who (@draw end_ch@) spend the money can see which part is evidence and which part is theirs to decide.
    draw end_ev

## Where next
end_page: true

    link end_again href lecture:18 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 400 font_size 28
    text end_count "18 of 18" x 500 y 330 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
